<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <title>Calculator</title>
</head>
<body>
  <h1>Calculator</h1>
  <input type="text" id="display">
  <button onclick="clearDisplay()">C</button>
  <button onclick="backspace()">←</button>
  <button onclick="clear()">clr</button>
  <button onclick="add()">+</button>
  <button onclick="subtract()">-</button>
  <button onclick="multiply()">×</button>
  <button onclick="divide()">÷</button>
  <button onclick="equals()">=</button>
  <script>
    let display = document.getElementById('display');

    function clearDisplay() {
      display.value = '';
    }

    function backspace() {
      const text = display.value;
      if (text.length > 1) {
        display.value = text.substring(0, text.length - 1);
      }
    }

    function clear() {
      clearDisplay();
      display.value = '';
    }

    function add() {
      const num1 = Number(display.value);
      const num2 = Number(prompt('Enter second number'));
      display.value = num1 + num2;
    }

    function subtract() {
      const num1 = Number(display.value);
      const num2 = Number(prompt('Enter second number'));
      display.value = num1 - num2;
    }

    function multiply() {
      const num1 = Number(display.value);
      const num2 = Number(prompt('Enter second number'));
      display.value = num1 * num2;
    }

    function divide() {
      const num1 = Number(display.value);
      const num2 = Number(prompt('Enter second number'));
      display.value = num1 / num2;
    }

    function equals() {
      const num1 = Number(display.value);
      const num2 = Number(prompt('Enter second number'));
      display.value = num1 === num2;
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>
```

**How it works:**

* The HTML code defines the calculator interface.
* JavaScript functions handle the click events for each button.
* The `display` variable stores the calculated result.
* When a button is clicked, it updates the `display` value.
* The `equals` function performs an equality check and sets the `display` value to `true` or `false`.

**Note:**

* This code does not handle invalid input, such as empty fields or non-numeric characters.
* The calculator uses `prompt` to prompt for the second number. This can be replaced with a different input method if desired.

